An arrow function expression (also called a _fat arrow_ to distinguish from a hypothetical `->` syntax in future
JavaScript) has a shorter syntax compared to function expressions and does not have its own `this`, `arguments`,
`super`, or `new.target`. Arrow functions are always anonymous.
Two factors influenced the introduction of arrow functions: _shorter functions_ and _non-binding_ of `this`.
